course: dictionary
lesson: 1588
title: Chauffeur: the paid driver
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A chauffeur is a person whose job is driving someone else's car, often for a rich or important person. Chauffeur can also be a verb: to chauffeur someone is to drive them around. Read the clues in the sentence to find the meaning. «He is cut off in the middle of "how very clev——" when he sees a chauffeur (a paid car driver) in leather gaiters» tells you the chauffeur is a paid car driver.
explain: The words around a word are its context. In «a chauffeur and two large country policemen are running after him!», the chauffeur is running after someone, so he is a person who is not in a hurry to stop and open a door for a passenger. Ask yourself what would fit in the sentence, then swap in your meaning, "a paid car driver", and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Parents sometimes joke that they are their children's «chauffeur» because they drive them to clubs and parties. Here chauffeur is a verb: the parent is driving the children around. The joke works because a real chauffeur drives people for a job, and the parent is doing the same job for free.
fact: A chauffeur is a paid driver of someone else's car.
fact: Chauffeur can be a noun (a chauffeur) or a verb (to chauffeur someone around).
fact: Phrases you may see: a uniformed chauffeur, a chauffeur-driven car.
fact: The word comes from French, and first meant a man who stoked the fire of a steam engine.
fact: Context clues help you work out a word: «The Badger tells the chauffeur he won't be wanted, and the friends bundle Toad inside.»
exercise: «He is cut off in the middle of "how very clev——" when he sees a chauffeur (a paid car driver) in leather gaiters.» What is a chauffeur? | A policeman from the country | A doctor who visits the sick | A friend who rides a bicycle | *A paid car driver
exercise: Parents joke they are their children's «chauffeur» because they drive them to clubs and parties. What does it mean to chauffeur someone? | To teach them to swim | To wake them early | To cook a meal for them | *To drive them around
exercise: Which language does the word chauffeur come from? | Spanish | Latin | *French | Greek
test: Which job is a chauffeur's? | Cooking meals for a family | Mending roads | Selling newspapers | *Driving someone else's car for them
test: Which sentence uses chauffeur as a verb? | A chauffeur is a paid driver | A chauffeur-driven car was parked by the gate | *Mum will chauffeur us to the swimming pool | A uniformed chauffeur waited outside
exam: What does «to chauffeur someone» mean? | *To drive them around, as a job or a favour | Repair their engine | Polish their shoes | Sleep in a car with them
